0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-25T12:39:34-07:00","id":1211507,"title":"","content":"Now, for the peoplewho optednt not talkm dwon to me, untfrottrunatr4ly the format that it is currently coming through as has no black anr t all","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-25T12:42:36-07:00","id":1211510,"title":"","content":"I have no idea if Isator is typoing out of rage, if his computer is glitching, or if we\u2019re witnessing a breakdown of the barriers between worlds right now. If you want I can send you the PDFs? I hope that\u2019s allowed since I\u2019m 100% sure you\u2019ve backed them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-25T14:03:50-07:00","id":1211557,"title":"","content":"All right then. All right then.<br /> <br /> My dear, sainted sister has loaned me her laptop for the moment, on which the document uploads in the manner that it was doing until just last night. I\u2019m going to work off of that for the time being. I\u2019m still a bit buzzed from how it was previously broken and\u2026 associated unpleasantness, but I\u2019m going to try and power on through.<br /> <br /> So, the additional Martial Arts that the book included. First is Golden Janissary Style: the description here has been made nice ad focused, describing how it\u2019s been passed down through generations of devil-fighting sages, trained with katas, footwork and meditation on light and shadow, and favoured by, ascetics, people who live among or adjacent to monsters or might regularly take part in the Wyld Hunt. The unarmed attacks are described as sweeping, and it favours the spear and staff as weapons. It\u2019s compatible with light armour, and complemented by both Occult, and Athletics for the improved mobility.<br /> <br /> The first Charm is Where-Is-Doom Inquisition, with which one can add their Occult to rolls for Join Battle, as well as Awareness rolls to detect hidden threats while in combat. When creatures of darkness oppose the roll, 1s on their rolls can add non-Charm dice to the fighter\u2019s rolls. The Charm doesn\u2019t specify which foes are dark, but the fighter will know that <i>somebody</i> is.<br /> Ah, I had almost forgotten that Mastery effects were a thing. Those who have it gain the benefit of being able to reflexively move one range band towards a creature of darkness if they win Join Battle against them and use the first turn to attack one. They can still take a normal movement action, and gain their Occult in dice of post-soak withering damage or one success on a decisive damage roll.<br /> <br /> There\u2019s a sidebar reiterating/clarifying the definition of creature of darkness (I can\u2019t recall if the core had one), and then Rotten Leaf Arrested to give the stylist\u2019s withering attacks a slightly increased Overwhelming and knock them prone if damaged, with enemies that are crashed being incapable of moving on their next turn altogether. When used against creatures of darkness, the Overwhelming bonus is half the stylist\u2019s Occult, and the withering damage roll gets an automatic success, and if crashed can neither move nor <i>attack</i> on the next turn.<br /> <br /> Cleansing Flame Strike adds a die of damage to decisive attacks and let them ignore two points of Hardness, with creatures of darkness giving the damage roll double 10s and taking aggravated damage, and then it\u2019s the Form Charm. When the Golden Janissary Form is assumed, the martial artist gains half their Strength as a bonus to their soak and dice to rushes, and gains one Initiative when a rush is successful. Against creatures of darkness, they also get increased Parry and their Occult added to soak as well, and an extra point to base Initiative whenever they land a decisive attack. It can be reflexively entered when a creature of darkness is decisively attacked, automatically granting the extra Initiative.<br /> <br /> Devil-Slaying Spear Dance can be used when a foe is incapacitated, to reflexively rush a different enemy with double 9s, without it counting as their movement action. If they rush a creature of darkness, 1s on the opposing roll grant the warrior Initiative. Mastery allows one to instead instantly move to close range of an enemy and reflexively make a decisive attack on them, and if <i>that</i> foe is incapacitated, the Charm can be applied again.<br /> <br /> Paralyzing Combustion Imbuement is used to make a decisive attack that cause a foe to erupt in melodious golden flames, penalizing their Stealth and increasing their wound penalties. If the penalty is risen above the target\u2019s Stamina, then the target becomes incapable of disengaging from close range to the martial artist so long as they remain lit. The light burns creatures of darkness, inflicting dice of aggravated damage that ignore Hardness every turn. The light lasts for the scene, or until the inflicted character crashes or incapacitates the one who did it to them. Masters are able to make their enemies incapable of disengaging from <i>any</i> range, while Terrestrials are limited to an effect that is broken simply by landing a decisive attack.<br /> <br /> Light-on-Dark Shield is used for a withering clash attack. It can\u2019t have more damage than the character\u2019s Overwhelming value (which they don\u2019t receive), but against creatures of darkness a successful clash inflict one die of Hardness-ignoring aggravated damage. For Terrestrials the clash counts as their attack for the round, and thus cannot be used if they\u2019ve already attacked.<br /> <br /> Finally there\u2019s Lone Spark Lights the Conflagration. A decisive attack can be made against an enemy suffering from Paralyzing Combustion, allowing the attack to include threshold successes to the damage roll, with the total allowed increased against creatures of darkness. When a creature of darkness is damaged, the light that the attack naturally emanates flares out to strike any other creatures of darkness within medium range, giving them one die of aggravated damage, plus one for every 10 rolled on the initial damage roll. Damaged enemies will also acquire the effect of Paralyzing Combustion Imbuement. Masters get a very strong bonus to the Charm; when they incapacitate a creature of darkness and reset to base, they can use Devil-Slaying Spear Dance to rush against all enemies struck by the Charm\u2019s aftereffect, making decisive attacks against them one after the other (I feel as though there\u2019s an inconsistency in how this describes resetting to base, but I\u2019d need to review some things). Terrestrials are instead limited to only being able to use the Charm once per scene, but can reset it by crashing enemies suffering from Paralyzing Combustion.<br /> <br /> I\u2019d say that Golden Janissary came out well. It\u2019s got some flash, some straightforward internal synergies, and I think it\u2019s sufficiently effective even against normal foes while having some nice boosts against creatures of darkness.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-25T14:30:49-07:00","id":1211566,"title":"","content":"My sister obviously (and entirely fairly) took her laptop back, so I'll have to wait until tomorrow before I can get a look at Mantis Style.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-25T19:07:31-07:00","id":1211648,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/dragon-blooded/1200127-isator-levi-reads-dragon-blooded-what-fire-has-wrought?p=1211505#post1211505\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">FDO UI SEEM KLIKE IMM IN A MOO DTO HAVE GAMES PLAYED WITH ,ME</div> </div> </div> </div>Hey, Levi, man, I just wanted to thank you for the hard work you've put into this analysis. I'll be honest, it was your read of Arms that inspired my own thread (which I've fallen behind on\u2026 &gt;.&gt;), and I've enjoyed this thread at least as much.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-25T19:33:16-07:00","id":1211656,"title":"","content":"Ah excellent; my laptop managed to pull itself together, and my documents are displaying again as normal.<br /> <br /> \u200bI am going to keep going until I either hit the end of the book, or collapse. I am going to be finished before the end of the Kickstarter.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-25T20:20:38-07:00","id":1211674,"title":"","content":"Okay, get a Mantis Toboggan reference out of my system from the outset: Mantis Toboggan is a persona that the character of Frank Reynolds occasionally adopts when he\u2019s impersonating a doctor in the hit sitcom It\u2019s Always Sunny in Philadelphia. It\u2019s funny because of the surreal absurdity of the name and its demonstration of the character\u2019s unhinged depravity, particularly in the contexts in which he employs it.<br /> <br /> Good, now that\u2019s out of everybody\u2019s way.<br /> <br /> I believe Lea was the one who said that he insisted upon another animal Style being included, because he didn\u2019t want them forgotten amidst all the flashy abstract Styles. Whomever was responsible, I appreciate the addition.<br /> <br /> Mantis Style is described as being based on grappling and rapid strikes (quite appropriate, considering the insect\u2019s hunting method). It combines offense and defence, using disabling joint holds and redirecting blows, and is placed into the book\u2019s context by being referred to as favoured by Immaculate monks as a means of subduing foes without killing. The unarmed strikes are a combination of strikes with hooked fingers, knees and elbows, as well as quite a variety of weapons: batons, seven-section staves, war fans, and the newly introduced nunchaku and kama. This is the first of the Styles in this book with no armour compatibility, and has no complementary Abilities.<br /> <br /> Iron-Arm Block starts the Style out with an application of its basic principle, granting additional Parry and inflicting onslaught on the one who attacked if they are successfully blocked. If the user is grappling, then a blocked blow doesn\u2019t reduce rounds of control, and Mastery means that the stylist does not suffer onslaught penalties for successfully blocked attacks.<br /> With Crushing Claw Technique, the Mantis is able to add an automatic success to the attack roll for a grapple, or other decisive attack, and is able to make a Martial Arts roll with bonus dice to establish control of a grapple, or add a limited number of 10s rolled on the attack as extra dice for the damage roll.<br /> <br /> The Form Charm comes very early. Adopting the characteristic intimidation stance of the mantis, the user gains increased Parry, and is able to add their Parry value to their natural soak or half of their Parry in Hardness for attacks that they failed to block (a nice representation of the image of outstretched arms getting in the way). When attacking enemies with lower Initiative or that are being grappled by them, they double 10s on decisive damage rolls. Mantis Form can be activated reflexively upon winning control of a clinch.<br /> <br /> Leaping Mantis Technique allows the stylist to rush an enemy with bonus dice (with increased range under circumstances that I can\u2019t fully parse until I reread the rush rules), and able to reflexively make a decisive attack or grapple if the rush brings them into close range of their target, with half of the threshold successes on the rush to the attack roll as bonus dice. If the rushed enemy doesn\u2019t do\u2026 something, then the stylist can make a decisive attack or grapple that is unblockable, with <i>all</i> of the rush\u2019s extra successes as bonus dice to the attack roll. Masters get a point of Willpower when they successfully land these attacks.<br /> <br /> Using Joint-Locking Technique, a grappled and restrained foe can have unsoakable withering damage equal to the attacker\u2019s Strength rolled against the enemy, with no cost in rounds of control if the enemy was crashed (with Masters able to convert Initiative gained from the withering attack into more rounds of control, on a 2:1 exchange ratio). While Grasping Claw Method is used to immediately disarm a foe upon grappling them, tossing their arms out to short range (with Mastery allowing one to add threshold successes from the attack roll into bonus dice on the control one).<br /> <br /> Last up are three at Essence 3. Grasping Mantis Defense sounds like a fairly self-explanatory name, aaaaand actually I was mistaken. I was assuming some kind of improvement to defence while grappling, but it\u2019s actually an ability to clash a close range attack with a grapple, with the successful clash adding extra non-Charm dice to the control roll. That makes more sense. Terrestrials are limited by the clash counting as their attack for the round*, and thus unusable if they\u2019ve already attacked.<br /> <br /> The charmingly named Joint-Breaking Attack allows a stylist to inflict a special savaging attack on a clinched enemy, rolling a difficulty 6 gambit that requires no attack roll. Success inflicts a single lethal health level of damage (against which Hardness doesn\u2019t apply), and breaks one of the target\u2019s limbs for a crippling injury, either imposing a -3 penalty on any actions that require two arms or causing a broken-legged person to treat all ground as difficult terrain. It also increases wound penalties by two for the remainder of the scene. A metric is given for how long the broken limb can take to heal. Martial artists the crash a grappled enemy with Joint-Locking Technique can activate the Charm reflexively, while Terrestrials can only use the Charm once per scene unless they reset it by expending three rounds of control over a grapple to do so.<br /> <br /> Last of all is Unfolding Retribution Strike, a signature finishing move of a rapid barrage of blows, which I suppose is a kind of representation of the highly efficient shredding mouth parts of the mantis. A fighter that is controlling a grapple and has at least 15Initiative can release the clinch to launch an unblockable, undodgable decivie attack, with each round of control given up adding 3 dice to the damage roll, and increasing the onslaught penalty by one. It can only be used once per scene, unless reset by crashing a grappled foe whose Initiative was higher than the fighter\u2019s. Mastery lets one add half of their expended rounds of control to base Initiative if their victim was incapacitated, while Terrestrials are limited in how many released rounds of control they give up by their Essence.<br /> <br /> Yes, having seen this one in full, I want to reiterate the value of it being here. It\u2019s nice to get reminders that Exalted martial arts don\u2019t always need to be all flash and special effects; that there\u2019s plenty of room for a fighting style based on efficient and specialized ways of hitting people.<br /> <br /> * I\u2019m suddenly struck by a bizarre image from how many times the Terrestrial keyword forces things like this to count as your action for the round, of Hermes Conrad telling off Dr. Zoidberg. Like, there was definitely at least one time that he told him that doing something counted as his lunch break or vacation or something. It\u2019s probably going too far to call Terrestrial Exalted the Dr. Zoidberg of Martial Arts\u2026 although I did characterize the keyword as depicting people who are earnestly trying so very hard\u2026","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-25T21:31:19-07:00","id":1211686,"title":"","content":"Now, White Veil Style. I\u2019m going to be upfront; I don\u2019t personally care for the whole schtick of talking around the White Veil Society existing, even if I like the society and its martial art. I get the significance of including it here, since it\u2019s the first instance in a new Edition, and it\u2019s going to be somebody\u2019s introduction to the concept, but I am not personally going to participate.<br /> <br /> With that out of the way, the White Veil Society is introduced as a loose-knit association of spies, assassins and martial artists spread through the Realm and elsewhere. Their motivation is not described, but I\u2019ve personally preferred it as more of a Freemason\u2019s kind of group, promoting certain values and mutually assisting one another, rather than being a conspiracy with a goal. They have their signature deadly martial art, that exists beyond the society due to students willing to pay vast sums for learning it and the occasional rival that has uncovered records of its techniques. It\u2019s widely employed at social gatherings, and is described as alternately being quick and quiet, and torturous and stretched out over weeks. It uses swift, precise attacks to hit pressure points, as well as garrotes and needles, is incompatible with armour, and complemented by Socialize and Stealth for obvious reasons.<br /> <br /> I said all of that while excising the endless negatives. I\u2019m willing to give the benefit of the doubt in principle, but the difficulty of actually reading this wording and figure out what it\u2019s saying would be grating under any circumstances, and right now is really wearing my patience thin. If this is continued throughout the Charms, I\u2019m going to be quite nettled.<br /> <br /> I like how garrotes can inflict either bashing or lethal damage, depending on the material.<br /> <br /> Okay good, the Charms look as though they\u2019re written sensibly. It starts with Bordsong Over Blades, allowing the stylist to conceal their attacks behind mundane motions. A successful decisive attack can be made to forego rolling damage (and thus, doesn\u2019t need to reset to base), while still getting to inflict poison from weapons and the harmful effects of Charms besides their improvement of actual damage rolls. This means that even realising an attacks has happened requires an Awareness roll against a potentially quite high difficulty by all onlookers, including the victim. Using the attack from concealment doesn\u2019t break stealth, and one can apply their Stealth successes as the difficulty of the Awareness roll if that would be better than the normal one. Mastery lets the Charm also inflict a penalty to the victim for resisting poison and other effects.<br /> <br /> There\u2019s a sidebar on how to deal with poison that drains Initiative outside of combat. I\u2019d say that needing to roll Join Battle to create a pool just for the poison is sensible, allowing it to still be fast-acting and worthwhile, without being either kind of worthless or too much better outside of combat.<br /> <br /> With Owl Clutches at the Night, the stylist gets to conceal failed attacks, forcing a similar kind of Awareness roll to the prior Charm, with attacks made from concealment maintaining stealth if nobody succeeds on the roll. Mastery gives the added benefit of receiving Willpower if nobody sees through you.<br /> <br /> Using Alehouse Memory Stance, one is able to adopt an innocent demeanour, adding base Guile as bonus dice to a Stealth roll, ignoring the penalty for attempting that in combat if nobody has yet seen them make an attack.<br /> <br /> The White Veil Form settles the fighter into a relaxed stance that improves their Evasion and Guile, removes the penalty for a flurry so long as it includes at least one Socialize action, and lets one substitute Manipulation for Dexterity in calculating Evasion or rolling to enter concealment, disengage or withdraw. Enemies that roll Join Battle in response to the martial artist need to roll with the lower of their Awareness or Socialize. The Form can be entered reflexively when a surprise attack is successful.<br /> <br /> With Blithe Unruffled Plumage, the fighter is able to apply their Guile + 1 to their natural soak and reduce the Overwhelming value of attacks, or else gain their Guile in Hardness. It includes bonuses provided to Guile by other sources, and lets the user conceal the attacks of others when they fail to hit or damage. Mastery allows one to steal Initiative equal to Manipulation from an attacker whose blow was concealed from all onlookers.<br /> <br /> The White Veil stylist can render onlookers Blinded by Laughter to attempt an ambush even while in plain view of a target, seeming completely innocent and rowdy until the blow lands. They get to roll Manipulation + Socialize with double 9s against the target\u2019s Resolve to establish the ambush, and if successful uses the result of that roll in place of Join Battle. Otherwise, it\u2019s a normal ambush or unexpected attack. It can only be tried once per scene, but is reset by landing a decisive attack on the same target without being noticed, although when reset, it can\u2019t be used on the same person. With Mastery, a successful ambush doubles threshold successes on a withering attack, or adds up to Guile threshold successes on a decisive one, for purposes of rolling damage (which includes bonuses to the Guile).<br /> <br /> Tickling the Dragon\u2019s Throat constitutes an attack to cut off air supply, allowing every two threshold successes on a decisive attack roll to impose a penalty to the victim\u2019s actions and Defence, up to a maximum of the user\u2019s Essence, lasting until the onslaught inflicted by the inciting attack wears off. Mastery lets one use any threshold successes left over when the penalty reaches its limit to increase the number of turns that the onslaught penalty lasts for, at an exchange of one turn per two successes.<br /> <br /> The Dragon Dies in Bed is a decisive attack to inflict a supernatural disease generated by the Martial Art (and also waiving the cost of Birdsong Over Blades if it\u2019s put in a combo with this Charm). The disease, referred to as Subtle Mercy, has a virulence based on the user\u2019s Manipulation, a high morbidity, and an interval of one day. In addition to the usual infliction of penalties or botches, it has magical effects at each intensity: minor causes weariness and lethargy, inflicting a slight fatigue penalty unless the victim sleeps for twelve hours each day, and greatly increases the difficulty to diagnose the disease; major causes the fatigue penalty to stack for every day that doesn\u2019t get the full sleep, up to a maximum of five, and damage cannot be healed naturally if one remains active during convalescence, while the disease becomes a bit easier to diagnose; defining increases the required sleep to twenty hours, and is also necessary to recover Willpower, while the disease is at its easiest to diagnose.<br /> <br /> Next up is The Dragon Dies Screaming, with which one\u2019s decisive attack becomes inherently poisonous, with the inflicted toxin able to inflict 4 Initiative damage per round, and Aggravated when crashed. It has a fairly long duration based on Essence and Manipulation, and inflicts a penalty equal to Manipulation. It also waives the cost of Birdsong Over Blades. Diagnosing the poison has increased difficulty, and the attacker can elect to delay the onset of the poison for anywhere between an hour and a number of weeks equal to their Essence, with an even higher difficulty to diagnose when delayed. It can only be used once per scene, reset by incapacitating a character while no other witnesses can connect it to you, and Mastery enhances the poison to be incapable of having its duration reduced below one round.<br /> <br /> Finally, there\u2019s The Dragon Succumbs, making a decisive attack (that can again be combined with a free instance of Birdsong) against an enemy suffering from poison, disease or both. A struck victim must make a Resistance roll against the morbidity of every disease that they\u2019re suffering from, with failure immediately advancing its level; if already Defining, it instead inflicts a level of aggravated damage that cannot be prevented. The attack also inflicts one interval of any poison automatically, as well as one for every threshold success on the attack roll, up to a maximum of the White Veil stylists\u2019s Essence + 1. If suffering from multiple venoms, the attacker chooses which one to apply. The poison\u2019s actual duration isn\u2019t lowered by the inflicted interval, but the Charm still can\u2019t be used to inflict more intervals than the poison actually has left. Mastery ensures that if a victim fails their roll against a disease\u2019s morbidity at the Defining level, they\u2019ll die after a number of turns equal to their own Stamina, unless somebody that has diagnosed the disease manages a Medicine roll against a rather hefty difficulty. While, in the Style\u2019s one and only Terrestrial effect, it can only be used against a target through an ambush attack.<br /> <br /> I find White Veil to benefit quite a bit from the consolidation of Martial Arts, giving it room for some especially impressive effects to really punctuate its place as an obscure but lethal method of attack. I especially like the pinnacle\u2019s method for making somebody\u2019s poison or disease fall upon them all at once.<br /> <br /> Okay, my eyes are starting to get squishy, so I\u2019m going to get in a few hours of sleep, before bombing through the rest.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T04:21:28-07:00","id":1211735,"title":"","content":"All right, I\u2019ve got just under six hours to go. It should be easier from herein.<br /> <br /> Sorcery doesn\u2019t waste any time with reiterated descriptions, and gets straight into new shaping rituals. I liked how the description of the Heptagram accommodates sorcerous initiation by virtually any path, but do think that it is a good idea to have a common method that represents the academy\u2019s scholarly approach. So, they can acquire sorcerous motes through consultation of texts or an appropriate mentor, channelling it through arcane formulae and well-practiced mudras, or harnessing some geomancy through elemental manses. The last one in particular I like, setting the precedent of being able to extract the Essence from a certain object, but I also like the capacity to measure the reaped motes from a stunt rating in the second one for the encouragement to describe what kinds of formulae or mudra one is relying on.<br /> <br /> Ah, of course, this is where the Geomantic Prodigy Merit that I saw mentioned in another thread would be. Fairly basic, one can add their Occult in dice to a roll for crafting regular architecture or geomancy every once in a while, that sounds helpful; even if the resulting structure is mundane and powerless, I like the idea of its form conforming to those standards as a means of giving verisimilitude to interesting buildings with holes through the middle of them or the like without needing to channel direct Essence. And the Merits for interacting with spirits play nicely into the old sense that Realm sorcery favours summoning.<br /> <br /> Now, the greatly hyped up Wanasaan Exorcist path, acquired through drowning in a frozen spring to see the truth of the world at the brink of death, and come back with a splinter of ice in your heart. They can gain sorcerous motes through burnt offerings and grave goods offered to them while they still live, suspending themselves in salt water until the brink of drowning again, or getting a ghost to return to the cycle of reincarnation. Although the last seems like it has among the highest capacities of any shaping ritual to store motes, I find the first to have one of the more interesting logics behind it, with one\u2019s soul having been made able to benefit from such sacrifices as though one was already dead.<br /> <br /> Obviously Eyes in Both Worlds (see ghosts by default) is very effective for their basic purpose, and I like the idea of having a Merit to proof oneself against drowning for that uncanny sense of how sorcery can transform. I can see somebody looking upon an exorcist who ritually drowned themselves once and now cannot die from breathing water, and thinking that they\u2019re pretty damned weird and wanting to give them a berth when possible.<br /> <br /> Now, I\u2019m hoping that since this is limited to Terrestrial Circle spells, it won\u2019t be too long. Beckoning That Which Stirs the Sky seems like a good place to start, since weather shaping has often been described as among the most basic functions and luxuries of sorcery (all the way back to Moorcock), and I think the system of difficulties for how drastic the change is and the inability of other sorcery to override it comes out well. It also gives the spell a certain visual twist with the Lovecraftian (at least as might be interpreted by Guillermo del Toro in Hellboy) image of chitinous limbs emerging from nowhere to alter the course of the atmosphere. If those are visible, that has to really freak out the normals. Now, how can it be distorted and what\u2019s the control benefit\u2026 ah, of course, for the latter, one can create weather that follows them, and for the former the altered weather in question is made into its opposite.<br /> <br /> Hmm, Floral Ferry, a maritime version of the Cinderella method of transportation, without needing to bother with all of that messy fauna because the wind and currents will provide your propulsion\u2026 or rather, magical propulsion in place of wind, even. The ship can sail itself using the creator\u2019s Occult to serve for necessary rolls, but its own magic is incapable of more complex functions such as chasing or fighting, requiring manual control to be taken of a ship not really made for it, and where its magical draw to intended destination becomes counterproductive. I can imagine an interesting scene of trying to conduct a naval battle by tacking not the wind, but the boat\u2019s intended destination.<br /> <br /> I had thought the highlighted words constituted different forms for the ship based on the seed material, but now, they\u2019re extra powers that Essence can be spent upon. The hull can be hardened to give additional levels of damage that it can take (that again fall back onto the regular hull once the duration has elapsed), they can leave behind trails of pollen in the ship\u2019s wake to gum up pursuer\u2019s so that they suffer a penalty based on the sorcerer\u2019s Intelligence (a nicely weird effect for an already odd spell), and being able to double the ship\u2019s Speed by having the sorcerer at the helm. Having it as one\u2019s control spell allows the ship to develop Evocations, and being distorted means that the interfering sorcerer can give it a new destination. Given how it isn\u2019t strictly described as ending if its summoner is no longer upon it, I like to think that a port or two in the world has an old tale about the time a strange ship that was covered in peach blossoms rolled into port with nobody upon it.<br /> <br /> Impervious Sphere of Water is fairly self-explanatory. I can see the balance requirements in making the full cover that it provides apply both ways, plus the difficulty of breaking the magically maintained surface tension in order to step out and perform an attack unimpeded. Mind, it\u2019s still water, so I can imagine that it can be swum to the top of to get through the top and attack from there, for whatever that\u2019s worth. And of course the sorcerer is limited to reflexive actions (with the benefit of moving if it\u2019s the control spell). I like the distortion effect of rendering the water unbreathable and a complete impediment to those within while being open to without, and not ending if the sorcerer takes action. It really speaks to what makes distortion more interesting than the old counterspells; it approximates the literary idea of dealing with a magical effect by devising and applying a contrary one, compared to the boring game idea of flicking a spell\u2019s off-switch.<br /> <br /> Keel Cleaves the Clouds enchants a ship to be able to sail upon vapours such as mist or fog, which probably won\u2019t generally carry you as high as the clouds, but can probably help to avoid a lot of traditional maritime dangers that one would strike within such a body of vapour. Oh wait, a reference is made to the potential to get as high as the clouds, fair enough. It\u2019s offset a bit by losing Speed bonuses from being on the water, such as for oars and currents, and if the spell or vapour runs out the ship descends gradually and safely\u2026 ahh, of course being able to continue sailing over land would be another benefit that it could confer. Sorcerers with this as their control spell gain the stange feature of being constantly surrounded by mist (which they can disperse at will but returns if their emotions are wrought or they\u2019re heavily focused), and have a natural capacity to walk themselves on mist or fog, with the traits of difficult terrain. Distortion makes the mist the ship is sailing on engulf it, obscuring the sight of those aboard, penalising sailing rolls and preventing foreseeing going over the edge of the bank.<br /> <br /> Sculpted Seafoam Eidolen (my God but aren\u2019t a lot of these related to water and sailing\u2026 unless there are going to be spells conforming to all five elements? So much for being shorter\u2026) lets one construct an almost indistinguishable replica of a person or animal or object out of the foam and reflections of water. Its imitations can only be basic, with simple speech and not really doing actions, although it passes any basic sensory tests. If the sorcerer is present, it can be puppeteered directly, otherwise it reverts to an uncomplicated pattern that nevertheless comes across as realistic action; not running a race, but probably not walking into walls either. It\u2019s possible to observe the simulacrum closely enough to figure out its illusory nature (against a difficulty of the successes rolled in creating it), especially if its being touched, and reading its intentions can also be used to realise that it\u2019s a fake person. Having it as a control spell lets it create multiple illusions per casting, and being distorted allows control to be seized over it. I\u2019ve often liked media depictions of magicians conjuring doubles of themselves from reflective surfaces, so I like the method of conveying (and expanding upon) that here.<br /> <br /> Now for something different, the spell Spoke the Wooden Face. A sorcerer can carve their own face into the bark of a tree, and thereafter can cast a spell to see and speak through it at any distance, able to make (unenhanced) Perception or influence rolls through the face\u2019s senses, although using it takes one\u2019s awareness away from their own body and surroundings (unless the spell is ended prematurely or the sorcerer takes damage). The sorcerer\u2019s presence can\u2019t be detected naturally until they give themselves away, and the faces can be destroyed to render the spell unusable. I like this one for the manner in which it incentivizes odd and potentially creepy actions; walking into a wood surrounding a reclusive Dynast\u2019s estate and seeing how every tree has been carved with one or more faces, or a person without context or comprehension coming across a sorcerer carving a face into a tree. Having it as the control spell lets one see and act through several faces at once, and distorting it cuts off the senses of a face, which also causes the sorcerer to lose a point of Willpower.<br /> <br /> To represent Earth, there\u2019s Stalwart Earth Guardian, a Ritual spell to draw, trace or carve a protective circle. From within, it provides a slight improvement to Defense or Resolve against attacks from without, but the real benefit is in what it does to those attackers; if within medium range of the ward\u2019s centre, the earth itself attacks them with an environmental hazard of decent danger and difficulty, which can also make the terrain harder to move through, and interfering with attempts to approach stealthily by imposing a penalty on movement actions and being noisy enough to awaken sleepers. If it\u2019s a control spell, apart from giving the sorcerer a strange earthy quality, their fingernails become hard enough to etch stone, functioning as an exceptional tool to Craft it, or write messages in Linguistics. Distortion turns the effects of the spell inward upon the ones hiding in the circle.<br /> <br /> Air gets Thunder Wolf Howl. This one has the kind of description of a spell that I like, invoking ancient forgotten forces and monsters, as the basis for designating a spot within medium range that will be subjected to a powerful thunderclap. It forces characters to roll Stamina + Resistance against a difficulty of the sorcerer\u2019s Essence or Intelligence (with battle groups penalised by their Size), with those that fall short suffering decisive bashing damage that ignores Hardness, equal to the sorcerer\u2019s Essence plus whatever Initiative is divided evenly between targets. It deafens those that take damage, requiring medical intervention to fix, and gives them a crippling penalty equal to how many 10s were rolled in the damage against them (reduced as each turn passes). It can also shatter appropriate nearby materials, as the Storyteller deems suitable. Sorcerers with it as their control spell increase its difficulty and base damage, and their voice gains a booming quality when they\u2019re overcome with strong emotion. I like this one for representing an unusual method, even by the mechanics of it, for using sorcery to attack.<br /> <br /> Hmm, I wonder if Unslakable Thirst of the Devil-Maw is elemental\u2026 hmm, opening a disgusting mouth in one\u2019s palm that leeches away moisture from air and flesh <i>might</i> be another one that falls under that element\u2026 Anyway, it lets Intelligence + Occult be rolled as an unblockable decisive attack against all characters in a cone out to medium range (also being undodgeable to battle groups), with every hit character taking dice of aggravated damage equal to the sorcerer\u2019s Intelligence + extra successes on the attack roll, minus the target\u2019s Stamina or Resistance, down to a minimum of one. It doesn\u2019t reset to base Initiative, and doesn\u2019t work on enemies who bodies would contain no moisture (while also not needing to subtract from the damage roll against victims with primarily liquid bodies). As a visual effect, it completely desiccates any normal plant life within its range. The stolen moisture gathers in a sphere above the sorcerer\u2019s hand for a few minutes, that otherwise functions as completely normal water; it can be placed in a container, drank, or used for Charms like Elemental Sheathe. Hence, the spell also has a mundane utility in gathering water when in all but the very least humid environs. If it\u2019s the control spell, then the spellbinder has a permanently open maw on one of their hands (probably best not their favoured one), which allows their unarmed attacks to have aggravated damage and the Piercing tag against enemies with bodily moisture, and dealing an additional die of withering or decisive damage against water elementals and similarly vulnerable foes. This is another one that is good for providing a sorcerous method of attack that is just plain damn weird (and presumably alters the actions of one with it as the control a little bit, assuming that they have to wear a glove most of the time).<br /> <br /> Finally, to let Fire get a word in, there is Virtuous Guardian of Flame. A burning sword can be drawn from the heart of the sorcerer, that functions as a protector to watch over them. When the casting is completed, the sword coalesces into a small wispy flame that follows them around, and can be concealed by directing it to an already lit lantern or torch that the caster carries. If the caster is attacked, the wisp reforms into a sword to apply a defend other action with a Parry of the sorcerer\u2019s Essence or Intelligence, +1. It makes its own rolls to notice possible threats, using a dice pool composed of its charge\u2019s Essence, Perception and Occult, and destroys anything wooden that it blocks, as well as inflicting a die of Hardness-ignoring lethal damage when parrying an unarmed or other natural attack. If its defence is overcome, it slows attacks down enough to increase the target\u2019s soak by their Essence, unless the attack was already unblockable.<br /> <br /> The fire sword can be damaged in its own right, having soak, Hardness and -0 health levels all based on calculations from the caster\u2019s Essence. It has equal Initiative to the sorcerer when they Join Battle, although purely for the benefit of interacting with withering attacks. If the sword is crashed, it dissipates for the length of a scene, but returns thereafter. If destroyed, it can\u2019t be summoned again until its original duration elapses. If it\u2019s a control spell, it can unlock Evocations, including for being wielded offensively, and distortion allows it to be stolen to provide protection for a scene to the usurper. One thing that I like in sorcery is weird little summonings, so this one plays in nicely.<br /> <br /> There are good spells here, both for providing examples to fill out the elemental interactions with Dragon Blooded Charms, and generally broadening the scope of what this option can do.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T06:33:03-07:00","id":1211767,"title":"","content":"Now, for the chapter on Heirloom Artifacts. After Arms of the Chosen, this one ought to be good.<br /> <br /> First a fiction in which we finally get to see Left Hand Chalima in action. Huh, I was not expecting her to be the sort who would set themselves up as a ruler, so I\u2019m already invested (particularly since she\u2019s dealing with the boring mundanity of administrative paperwork). I particularly like the idea of her having a husband who offered to perform that work for her that particular evening, as well as the idea that she turned him down.<br /> <br /> Heh, also the idea that, as the new boss, she\u2019d redesign the prior dynasty\u2019s mausoleum to something that she viewed as more tasteful and less ostentatious, while still constituting an impulse to actually be respectful of the ghosts; plus the way that they griped about it like troublesome relatives.<br /> <br /> Oh, and she\u2019s had a prior husband that she\u2019s directing a prayer to. That makes sense; her current one is probably also mortal, and she\u2019s definitely more than fifty years old, so she could have conceivably outlived at least one prior one. I like an Exalt being depicted as having a relationship with a mortal spouse such as this; where the bond and memory of one might endure even past their natural death (possibly literally if her husband did actually leave a ghost). Also the way that she makes a wry observation to comparisons between the ease of starting and stopping fires. Yes, she\u2019s got a kind of Conan-vibe to her, with the distress over dealing with an enemy that can oppose without fighting, I like it.<br /> <br /> Oh, her husband actually did leave a ghost, and he shows up for what is apparently the first time. I\u2019m guessing that praying over the difficulties faced by the increase of Anathema activity is a big enough deal to prompt that.<br /> <br /> Aww, and he prompts her to try and get her old band together.<br /> <br /> This is a lovely little story. I already liked her design, but I think that Chalima might be my favourite out of the signature Kinship.<br /> <br /> So, Heirlooms of Power. There\u2019s a sidebar briefly reiterating what Resonance and Dissonance is, and then\u2026 straight into the write-up of an Artifact. Huh. Let me just check ahead a bit\u2026 I see, there isn\u2019t a special description of a general system or approach for Artifacts passed down through a family, the chapter is just an assortment of Artifacts with their Evocations (which may still be described in a manner referring to family legacy). That\u2019s not a bad thing, mind, since Artifacts are pretty great, but it does complicate my approach\u2026<br /> <br /> Okay, handle this in the same manner as Arms; don\u2019t analyse each device down to its individual Evocations, just a broad overview of history, concept, and any standout powers. They each get about a paragraph. Nice and simple.<br /> <br /> Calumny: a green jade wrackstaff, previously the weapon of a member of House Cynis that went around with the identity of an infuriating thief to hound the Great Houses. It\u2019s made from bamboo from the Imperial Mountain, banded with brown-speckled green jade, and passed down through generations of the House\u2019s blackmailers. It has a basic function of being collapsible into a short, easily concealed rod, and is powered by secrets, with intensity based on the kind of influence that would get somebody to reveal it publicly (which also means that its value to the staff can shift over time, as the secret becomes less fraught). It\u2019s got powers to find secrets, weaponise them against opponents, improve defence against somebody with them\u2026 ohh, I like the image of the pinnacle to convert a person\u2019s secret into an actual poison that can kill them, that\u2019s a strong image. God I love Artifacts now.<br /> <br /> Daring Venture: green jade powerbow, a birthday present to V\u2019neef from her father, based on her tendency to sneak off into the woods for her own explorations and a brief escape from stifling normal life. She struck music from it to tame a giant monster wolf, and it became bonded to the weapon\u2019s legend (that\u2019s a very nice spin on the usual narrative as a basis for its power, as well as interesting insight into herself). It\u2019s described as having a yearning for adventure and discovery in the wilderness (as well as summoning the wolf, Hundred Rivers), and the busy V\u2019neef often lends it to children with a similar inclination to herself to chart the future of the House. It has powers to tame beasts (or enhance familiars), guiding one to new places, and gaining Hundred Rivers as a familiar that will immediately travel with all haste to their side (plus a description of the rather lovable monster), as well as enhancing the Charm to train the wolf and empowering his loyalty to his master.<br /> <br /> Okay, I see the significance of heirlooms now; it\u2019s not a special form for Evocations to take, it\u2019s a context in which an Artifact\u2019s legend develops, and the legacy conferred by it being something that can be passed from one Exalt to another for generations. Daring Venture is the matriarch\u2019s powerbow, and the House\u2019s connection to an icon of her legend (which might endure even after she herself dies, since Hundred Rivers already seems to be far longer lived than a normal canid). There\u2019s a context of respect and honour to being entrusted with it, with commensurate disgrace if it is lost and motivation to get it back. That\u2019s good; it roots these Artifacts to the Dragon Blooded, and gives the write-ups a distinct flavour from Arms, with its focus on a hoary past.<br /> <br /> {checks back} Also, Evocations that unlock automatically for House scions.<br /> <br /> Faith\u2019s Pillar: white jade grand goremaul made for Mnemon\u2019s son Beral, who established their standard for proselytization, by respectful Mountain Folk. Beral carried it into battle against the monstrous enemies of the Jadeborn, and when he fell in battle they returned it to his family, where it gets passed to family monks. I like the description of it being carved in emulation of the Imperial Mountain, with symbols of the four Directions on each face, and how it\u2019s otherwise a stark, geometric shape; a nice sense of Immaculate minimalism still making for a stylish Artifact. Its power centres around a chosen Principle of religious belief or social order, either enhancing that belief or using it to enhance the wielder\u2019s stolidness and force, or punishing those who violate it. Also, being able to coat the head with a clod of compacted earth or stone to beat on people with the strength of your conviction, as well as culminating in a power to seal a crashed enemy in a suffocating tomb (especially difficult to break out of if they\u2019ve violated the Pillar\u2019s guiding Principle).<br /> <br /> Heavenly Typhoon: a much storied (and thus obscured) blue jade infinite chakram of House Ledaal, often said to have been made by an ancient hero inspired by the force of the hurricane to make a weapon to strike down Anathema, actually made in the First Age by a Solar for her spymaster Terrestrial to uncover plots and strike down the corrupt and the blasphemous, eventually used by the original owner\u2019s granddaughter to kill the Solar who made it, and ultimately found by a distant descendent from Ledaal. Its blue jade design has a very pretty addition in an orichalcum inlay that alternately looks like a jagged lightning bolt and bared dragon fangs, depending on the angle. Its power is built around a notion of condemning enemies, which despite the name, doesn\u2019t constitute a moral judgement, just an enemy struck by its first Evocation (which has no cost against creatures of darkness). The powers from this can assault or harry those condemned by it, with a pinnacle of being able to cast a storm of lightning-wreathed chakrams to hit numerous condemned characters at once with a special withering attack.<br /> There\u2019s also a short sidebar on the Realm\u2019s disdain for mixed-material Artifacts, but not so extreme that they don\u2019t use them.<br /> <br /> Pyre of Legions: red jade grand daiklave from House Cathak, initially owned by a member of the House that was regarded as unaccomplished and unexceptional despite her determination and disdained for being anti-social, until giving her life in a spectacular display that cost an enemy greatly. I\u2019m assuming that she possessed a mundane weapon that was used as the basis in forging the daiklave, which has the striking detail of its first wielder\u2019s burning handprint on its hilt. It carries the memory of Cathak Falu\u2019s last thoughts, conferring a Defining Principle of giving anything to protect comrades to those who attune it, that cannot be weakened or altered. Its powers are based on standing at the side of comrades against overwhelming odds, and protection of those comrades (which has a nice synergy with the character of Fire Aspected Melee), with the self-sacrifice angle coming from many having a cost in aggravated damage. I think there\u2019s something particularly special in its pinnacle Charm; letting the wielder spend Willpower instead of health levels on the prior Evocations, and treating them as though they have the worst wound penalty for the sake of a power that accounts for that. The image that the spirit of somebody who sacrificed themselves might live on by offering an alternative to that for a future generation, that exemplifies what is so good about Artifacts now, not just for power, but for personality, and ideals, and (as especially fitting for the Dragon Blooded) legacy.<br /> <br /> Sea\u2019s Verdict: a black jade longfang of a Peleps judge, carved from the stone that was used in sacrificial rites to an elemental by a cabal of priests that attempted to murder (and then bribe) the judge. It has the form of a trident (which distinctly gives it the Disarming tag) carved engraved with silver serpents and moonsilver prongs, and typically wielded by the House\u2019s poets and pirate-hunters to destroy criminals and other evildoers, although the Artifact itself is indifferent to such ideals or romanticism. It has its own anima banner that grows as motes are spent on powers (even besides its own Evocations) to enhance its attacks and parries, causing its form to grow wild as the engravings dissolve, building it to add the Smashing tag, and applying the indiscriminate pressure of the sea to batter and crush opponents. This is an interesting example of an Artifact contrary to the norm, where its legend is informed less by the nature of those who created it and more by the unfathomable and unfeeling quality of the force from which it was made.<br /> <br /> Smiling Razors: a pair of red jade short daiklaves that were bestowed on a monk from House Sesus, who grew too enamoured of the violence of Wyld Hunts while disdaining monastic serenity, very publicly extricating herself from the Order by burning off her robes with her anima and profaning an alter, openly thumbing her nose at the Order with challenges to monks and drawing Dynasts to apostasy with such extreme audacity that she was actually considered to qualify as Anathema, and ironically subjected to the Wyld Hunt herself. Her own cousin led the Hunt that killed her, and claimed the weapons (contrary to his own temperament) to make a public show of cleansing their dark history, so they could be added to the arsenal of his House. The Razors have powers to attack in secrecy, adopting the demeanour of a shadow in firelight, as well as improving infiltration and bestowing painful, burning wounds.<br /> <br /> Vengeant: a black jade set of razor claws in the possession of House Iselsi, forged from the remains of previous members of the House\u2019s arsenal on orders from the Scarlet Empress as a show of force, magnanimity, and reminder of their new status, which they adopted as a reversal of weapons to create dynasties into one that would end them. Its powers grow from judging an enemy to be despised, one who is hated to a particular member of the House or has a personal or ancestral connection to having wronged them, to launch secretive and disabling attacks (with a nice emphasis in the attack names and effects of targeting the other elements specifically), building up towards inflicting greusome deaths that leave a mark on those that discover their aftermath.<br /> <br /> Horizon Cleaver: blue jade grimcleaver (and first four-dot Artifact), another one dating to the very end of the First Age, made by a Sidereal inspired by the powerful storms created by battling Exalted in the Usurpation to be given to a Dragon Blooded lieutenant. It\u2019s haft is banded in steel and black rubber, with curved twin blades inlaid with red jade poetry to extol the virtue of the Usurpation, and in battle is wreathed with blue lightning. It passed down through the Shogunate to be wielded by one of Tepet\u2019s sisters in the siege of the Imperial City, and then in service to the Realm through the new House, to be lost track of after the campaign against Kaneko. Its power is based on unleashing powerful blasts of lightning that strike indiscriminately, that can become increasingly tamed as the wielder\u2019s power over it grows.<br /> <br /> Imprimatur: a white jade daiklave that the Scarlet Empress initially gifted to Nellens, and then bestowed upon the House elevated in his name (rather than the ones formed around his Exalted children by her), often carried when members of the House go into tense negotiation with other Houses, who view its existence as an insult. Imprimatur\u2019s power emerges from the Renown that it bestows upon those Nellens scions that wield it, with powers based on elevating the stature of that wielder and breaking down and humiliating those that oppose her (including in certain social engagements), building up to a pinnacle that both improves the enhancement that Renown provides to them, and allows them to draw strength from special messages written on the blade by the Empress, such as strong powers to enhance allies or deprive foes.<br /> <br /> Perdurant Vault: a white jade thunderbolt shield created by an occultist of Ragara in the early days of the House, made with the aid of a bargain with a queen of the Underworld. It\u2019s described as a large shield strung with an obsidian chain, made to be ornate so that it would not be out of place in high-society fashions, with a polished surface occasionally revealing a hidden city in its depths. While used in occult experiments of the House\u2019s high society, it\u2019s primarily employed by socialites, bureaucrats and politicians to shield them in the intrigues of Dynast society. Hence, it provides various powers to aid one in social interactions, protect them from clandestine and corrupting attacks such as poison or disease, and preserve them against other influences. As one gets deeper into its powers, they can benefit from hiding or banishing parts of themselves in the City of Opal Unstained, which can ultimately be entered so that they can be interacted with for special insights, or confronted in order to excise them.<br /> <br /> Not really much to say about the chapter in summary, other than it being another exceptional suite of magical weapons.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T07:56:03-07:00","id":1211802,"title":"","content":"Final chapter. This shouldn\u2019t take long.<br /> <br /> First a fiction returning us to Righteous River, as she deals with news that four Anathema are present on the sacrosanct Blessed Isle. I like the dynamic here with a mortal member of a Great House acting on their behalf; he might not have the power, but he can assert some of the attitude if he\u2019s on business for one of the Exalted.<br /> <br /> Ah, so as of the setting\u2019s year zero, River is no longer in the Immaculate Order, through a combination of guilt for having survived where several shikari died, and a wish to marry a woman. Aaaand one of the escaped Anathema is the one that was captured alive in the opening fiction. Makes sense. That she has a family also means she fears retribution, so she asks them to go into hiding while she, too, gets the band back together. That group probably has some interesting times ahead.<br /> <br /> River\u2019s fiction isn\u2019t quite the insight into an Exalt in the Order that I would have expected, but I find it a compelling narrative in its own right.<br /> <br /> So, Chapter Ten, Princes of the Earth. How shall I approach it\u2026<br /> <br /> Fairly minimalistic, at this late stage. Comment only upon character traits and stories that seem particularly interesting, and any QC renditions of Charms that might hint at distinct powers.<br /> <br /> Hmm, there\u2019s a new Imperial bureaucrat in the form of Amon Mora, a patrician in charge of the daily functioning of the Imperial palace (including safeguarding its treasure, files and arsenal), and descendent of a dispossessed Shogunate gens. He\u2019s highly placed, but keeps apart from Dynastic politics, having no Exalted children or family, and thus a distinctly lone Terrestrial. He doesn\u2019t quite have an agenda regarding the Throne, so much as a very reserved standard for offering support. Oh, and he stands as a nice bit of asexual representation. Let\u2019s see, usually accompanied by a unit of Palace guards\u2026 and possesses the Artifact that is the First Imperial Seal, used both mundanely to seal documents in the Empress\u2019 name and the actual key to the palace; its holder or an appointed representative needs to greet visitors so that the sorcerous defences won\u2019t regard them as intruders (I can see that as having given Mora power to control the Empress\u2019 appointments, keeping certain petitioners waiting), as well as literally opening certain inner chambers. Hmm, unless when it says that people of the Empress\u2019 blood are automatically welcome it means anybody descended from her in any degree\u2026 still useful. It also has an Evocation for improving defensive prowess against intruders.<br /> <br /> Berit is one of the daughters of the Scarlet Empress who achieved great battlefield acclaim, but was distinctly never granted a House of her own despite publicly expressing a desire for one. Berit led a force to fight a particularly troublesome Lunar, whom she personally slew in single-combat, after which she still wasn\u2019t granted a House despite all other honours and accolades, resulting in an estrangement from her mother and self-imposed exile to a marginal island prefecture. She lives in almost complete isolation as a scholar, and is rumoured on the island to be responsible for disappearances for sinister purposes. With the disappearance of the Empress, many Dynasts want to win her favour, since she\u2019s still an acclaimed general, and if she can, she\u2019d look to acquire the throne for herself. Children of the Empress who lacked Houses were always an interesting background detail, and it\u2019s nice to see one placed at the foreground with some baggage and a chance to shake up the power structures of the Dynasty. She\u2019s also at about the height of power that typical Exalted could reach.<br /> <br /> Heh, I like her Major Principle regarding hard work. A Water Aspect ought to be so salty.<br /> <br /> Yeeah, for the time being, I\u2019m only going to skim the quick character Charms. If Mnemon still has greater than Essence 5, I\u2019ll examine hers more closely, but otherwise they can wait.<br /> <br /> Cathak Cainan. I mostly know his story already, between some old elements of the backstory with his brother, and the new relationship with Cathak herself. He\u2019s a solid and high-minded authority in the Realm, and due to advanced age prefers to play the role of queenmaker. He\u2019s still sexy, but can feel himself slowing down. And while he\u2019s fair and honourable, he is so according to his own terms, which aren\u2019t exactly accommodating to satrapies (although still extends to an aversion for duplicity).<br /> <br /> Cynis Cogen is a grandson of the House founded, who after a couple centuries of living according to the House ethos and serving as a spy, grew bored of the hedonism and responded to losing his wife to old age by turning to the Immaculate Order in a search for meaning. He became an itinerant monk to travel as a teacher and searcher for heresy, applying his intelligence gathering skill for the Order\u2019s benefit. He\u2019s a powerful fighter, enjoying battle and holding nothing back as he doesn\u2019t expect to live long anyway. He also still has a lingering connection to the Dynasty through his daughters and old friends, which draws him to the periphery of intrigues.<br /> <br /> Ledaal Kes is another with a familiar story, a child prodigy with a much repeated story of playing Gateway against the Scarlet Empress in his youth, and Exalting extraordinarily young, creating a drive to excel in all he did. He\u2019s widely popular and has a taste for the finer things available to Dynasts, and is married to his longtime friend and rival Ragara Szaya, with whom he has created numerous strange inventions, and a mutually open relationship owing to their respective sexualities. In public, he\u2019s an official of the Treasury in the inner circle of minister Bal Keraz, tasked with keeping track of currency flow and counterfeiting (a kind of Exalted Sir Isaac Newton), and is secretly an agent of the All-Seeing Eye. He\u2019s rooted a bit in the Dynasty\u2019s adjustments with a bit of resentment for being subject to sexist remarks, but overall uses being underestimated to his advantage. His primary motivation isn\u2019t precisely to a higher loyalty, so much as the intellectual stimulation that comes from treating everything like a puzzle to solve.<br /> <br /> Ah, I\u2019ve wanted to see Mnemon, even if much of her story already seems to have been provided in the write-up of her House. Hmm, I feel as though there\u2019s a slight contradiction in describing her as <i>older</i> than four hundred, but never mind that for now. Her bloodline is impeccable, her manses are renowned, and the prestige of her House is among the Realm\u2019s finest. Many think of her as already being close to the next Empress, and few are happy about it for how ruthless and unyielding she is, as well as her litany of grudges.<br /> <br /> Oho, that\u2019s a fun new detail; in First Edition, she took pride in her birth having no omens, in contrast to Ragara\u2019s meteor shower, whereas now her birth was heralded by an earthquake. But the story of her Exalting in later teenage while sparring with a hidden Sidereal sifu remains the same. It still has the story of Ragara\u2019s attempted poisoning via suit of armour, but it also seems to suggest that the Versino did not actually dramatically fall, Mnemon just fled to the Immaculate Order because it wasn\u2019t safe, and from there developed training in warfare (another newer detail) and sorcery. She\u2019s got a reputation for hardly being defenceless even when not bearing obvious arms, including the old idea of often having a retinue of invisible demons. She\u2019s immensely powerful, but still power hungry, and still carries the Emerald Thurible.<br /> <br /> She\u2019s a devout and sincere adherent of the Immaculate Order, having funded construction of monasteries, had a hand in selecting the Mouth of Peace, and either financed Wyld Hunts or personally led them. But she still has a possible tendency to follow the principles of the Philosophy more in letter than spirit. She\u2019s also got a practice of having private audience with House members who join the Order, to both congratulate them and instruct them on how to reconcile loyalties to both institutions.<br /> <br /> Let\u2019s see, apparently never married, but numerous consorts and lovers, carefully selected for pedigree and power. Huh, it\u2019s nice that she actually lavishes affection on her lovers, even while she\u2019ll be trying to find marriages for them that suit her agendas.<br /> <br /> And she\u2019s an especially hands-on matriarch, deeply invested in the personal lives of descendents. Still, in keeping with her revised character, she\u2019s noted to offer wisdom and comfort in addition to scolding and scorn, although she probably has little mercy for those who go against her. Hehehe, I like the idea of her going sleepless nights to plan a descendant\u2019s marriage or counsel a nervous child, that nice blend of controlling and affectionate. I like the overall image that members of the House can see a less severe side to her than the Dynasty as a whole does, even if it\u2019s still one that is an admirable and fearsome pillar of power. Plus an acerbic sense of humour, you can\u2019t go wrong with that.<br /> <br /> Heh, after all of that, why not let her still have a certain view of the throne as her birthright? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Just a quick observation of the technical write-up: I expected that it was possible that Mnemon would be brought down to Essence 5. Not just because of the revised principle of what Essence constitutes, but because of notes when the whole attitude towards Essence was first brought up; the question of what higher Essence actually brought and gave to a character. For all Mnemon\u2019s gravitas, she was never exactly presented as a figure with grander powers outside of her sorcery, so better to keep her more on the level of \u201cpinnacle of expected Exalted power\u201d. Thus, there wouldn\u2019t be any special powers in her Charms, so those can be glided past.<br /> <br /> And I like her Intimacies and all, including what holds equal billing as Defining to her.<br /> <br /> Huh, the Emerald Thurible has a couple of Evocations, one in expected spirit binding, the other a nice image of making demon smoke to reduce costs to materialize.<br /> <br /> Ahhh dammit, I have no time\u2026 I don\u2019t want to bomb through the rest, but I want to post up before the Kickstarter\u2019s deadline, as I just want to review my pledge to ensure that everything is in order.<br /> <br /> If I hadn\u2019t lost a day\u2026 well, can\u2019t be helped. I\u2019ll return to finish shortly.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T07:59:56-07:00","id":1211804,"title":"","content":"Wait... never mind, I got faulty information on how British Summer Time corresponded to Irish time. There's still another hour to go. More than enough.<br /> <br /> Back in a tick.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T08:42:59-07:00","id":1211822,"title":"","content":"Now, Ragara Benoru, another new character. An unusually morally focused Dynast growing up (particularly for her House), she distinguished herself in youth by taking part in a peasant uprising, lending the rebels in a manner that kept them against responding legions. She was offered clemency in return for surrender, and the Empress regarded her as being ideal for the magistracy. By the time of the setting, she\u2019s served for decades, with several prestigious cases against corruption and conspiracy that have made her widely storied in the Realm (to the disapproval of authorities). Yeah, even I can often forget about the magistrates, so it\u2019s a good idea to include at least one in this section.<br /> <br /> The renown she has gives Benoru an advantage in the uncertain times, which she leverages to often place herself in the homes of Dynasts in which it would be inexpedient to murder her, or allow another to do so, while she still fulfils her duties. But she doesn\u2019t interpret that duty as maintaining order for the benefit of the corrupt Dynasts, and has a potential to galvanize the people into further rebellion, another interesting addition to the possible dynamics of civil war (along with the chapter fiction to suggest patricians seizing the Imperial City). The text might not be presenting her as a candidate for empress, but I\u2019m either reading between the lines or applying my own knowledge of history when I see something in her reminiscent in many Chinese peasants who died as sovereigns.<br /> <br /> Heh, \u201chare was once a dragon\u201d, nice word play.<br /> <br /> Huh, and she\u2019s also married to a woman in House Ledaal. I\u2019d suppose that\u2019s unusual for magistrates, but if she\u2019s still technically a Dynast\u2026 in any case, I like the note on how her typical demeanour reminiscent of a peasant shocks many who hear her speak in a thunderous, commanding voice when performing duties as a magistrate. Plus her having a Minor Principle of repeating peasant superstitions despite protesting that she doesn\u2019t believe in them.<br /> <br /> Ahh, good oul\u2019 Sesus Nagezzer. What\u2019s he been up to? The outset is about the same as it ever was, with his Throne of Roses (although I think the derisive name might be new), and his retinue of the scum of the earth. One thing that I like are the emphases in his physical description; not just his grooming, but how in addition to being quite fat, he\u2019s very tall and surprisingly powerful (while his leg is still maimed). I even see an element of making his appetites more impressive than off-putting. Plus the idea that he\u2019s a very reserved man at social events.<br /> <br /> Still has the martial backstory (including how the wound came from a powerful spirit), although I feel as though the idea that he built himself up as a paragon of martial prowess has more bravado than he did in First Edition. Given what is emphasized in the Immaculate Order now, it\u2019s interesting to consider that his shift in focus might have come less from being totally incapable of resuming his prior standing, and more in growing to see the value of an alternative path (even if full recovery was still in question). It combines interestingly with the House\u2019s overall tendency towards spycraft now. So he\u2019s got his entourage of hangers-on to obscure his agents and petitioners, as well as having a cousin of poor standing direct mercenary activities that are presumably beneficial to the Realm.<br /> <br /> Huh, Essence 4, he\u2019s actually rather powerful now. And has quite a few skills to make rolls on. I like the name of his weapon, and among his Intimacies think the more interesting are the contrast between distrusting his House and trusting the Sesus cousin that works under him.<br /> <br /> All right, final character, Tepet Ejava, the Roseblack. First off described as somebody who disdained House politics to serve the Realm, complicating her status as a possible saviour of Tepet. Still has the noteworthy background story of having been born on the battlefield, as well as some elements of her distinctly violent Exaltation story (in the Aspect book, she recalled briefly thinking that she had become a Lunar). I can\u2019t recall if \u201cthe Roseblack\u201d was ever put into context, but here it\u2019s given as a derisive nickname from an incident in which he destroyed a teacher\u2019s treasured rose garden (the monster!*), which she decided to lean into as a show of humility, being carried through her achievements until it was a mark of pride. Heh, I like that as another kind of thing that plays into the Caesar comparisons, given the origins of that name.<br /> <br /> The battle of Futile Blood upended her worldview with the major defeat to the Realm and loss of so many family, friends and comrades, so with the dire straight the House is in, even ones that are jealous of her would sooner have her prowess leading the one, inferior legion that they were saddled with. Ejava is said to have refined the Vermillion Legion significantly in her years in command, clearing up some more old ambiguities about its status. She\u2019s described as a consummate officer, and one that has distinctly put off marriage to her House\u2019s consternation. She\u2019s mentioned to have a single close-friend and confidante, one Ragara Nova, prefect for Chanos, but otherwise rather socially withdrawn and more at home commanding battle. She\u2019s finally described with her iconic features, her red hair (described as being of the same kind of as Scarlet Empress) and her green jade panoply.<br /> <br /> Aaaand that\u2019s everybody. I\u2019m just going to quickly look through the ending fiction.<br /> <br /> Oh, wait, this is covering every member of the Kinship individually? I\u2019m going to post this now, and come back for the denouement after I\u2019ve reviewed my Kickstarter pledge.<br /> <br /> <br /> * And I\u2019m not being ironic; I have a distinct dislike for people\u2019s precious possessions being destroyed.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":158,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","user":"Isator Levi","date":". 04-26-2018, 12:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T08:58:57-07:00","id":1211835,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/dragon-blooded/1200127-isator-levi-reads-dragon-blooded-what-fire-has-wrought?p=1211822#post1211822\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">All right, final character, Tepet Ejava, the Roseblack. First off described as somebody who disdained House politics to serve the Realm, complicating her status as a possible saviour of Tepet. Still has the noteworthy background story of having been born on the battlefield, as well as some elements of her distinctly violent Exaltation story (in the Aspect book, she recalled briefly thinking that she had become a Lunar). I can\u2019t recall if \u201cthe Roseblack\u201d was ever put into context, but here it\u2019s given as a derisive nickname from an incident in which he destroyed a teacher\u2019s treasured rose garden (the monster!*), which she decided to lean into as a show of humility, being carried through her achievements until it was a mark of pride. Heh, I like that as another kind of thing that plays into the Caesar comparisons, <b>given the origins of that name.</b></div> </div> </div> </div>Could you elaborate here a bit? I feel like I'm missing something...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Storyteller Vault releases!<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/269728/Sorcerous-Ways-Mystic-Martial-Arts\" target=\"_blank\">Sorcerous Ways: Mystic Martial Arts</a> - Punch people for magic power!<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/252892/These-Mean-Streets\" target=\"_blank\">These Mean Streets</a> - Advise and systems for street-level punk-noir Vampire larp","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/169-wastevens","name":"wastevens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar169_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"939"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T09:18:59-07:00","id":1211842,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wastevens</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/dragon-blooded/1200127-isator-levi-reads-dragon-blooded-what-fire-has-wrought?p=1211835#post1211835\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Could you elaborate here a bit? I feel like I'm missing something...</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bThe full name of the famous general of the late Roman Republic was Gaius Julius Caesar. In ancient Roman naming conventions, one could have three names; the personal name (in this case, Gaius), the names of the family or gens (here Julius), and the cognomen, which is basically a kind of hereditary nickname; an ancestor receives a cognomen for some distinct trait or achievement, and it persists through the family line. For example, Caesar's contemporary and rival, Gnaeus Pompeius Magnus, his cognomen meaning &quot;the Great&quot;, bestowed upon him by the dictator Sulla in recognition of his military achievements. Or Publius Cornelius Scipio, given the additional and personal cognomen Africanus, given to him after his victory over Carthage.<br /> <br /> \u200bThe exact origin of the Caesar cognomen is disputed; Pliny the Elder asserted that it came from an ancestor born by Caesarean section (coming from the verb caedere, to cut), whereas a Roman text from about two hundred years after he died suggested that it either came from caesaries (meaning hairy, suggesting an ancestor with an especially think head of it), oculus caesiis (referring to bright grey eyes), or for killing an elephant (or caesai, in a North African language) in battle. That Julius Caesar issued coins depicting elephants suggests that he favoured the last explanation, whereas the one about being hairy would be particularly ironic due to his own receding hairline (of which he was reportedly quite self-conscious).<br /> <br /> \u200bSo who knows; perhaps whatever &quot;Roseblack&quot; is pronounced as in Old or High Realm could eventually become the basis for a number of ruling titles, as Caesar became for Rome, transitioning to kaiser for German speaking countries, and tsar for Russia.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T10:00:11-07:00","id":1211863,"title":"","content":"Now, the last fiction to close the book.<br /> <br /> Starts with Righteous River, engaged with an old tic of massaging her hand after writing a number of letters, and speculating that it is exaggerated by knowing that the Anathema that her Hearth captured when she got the tic is on the loose, and reflecting (perhaps even flashing back) to that Wyld Hunt. She\u2019s already gotten Eshuvar on board with her plan, and has faith in the rest of her Kin coming through, thinking that the Solar is their responsibility for having been brought to the Blessed Isle by them, and bitterly attributing having taken him alive to pride, while resolving to deal with the consequences of their actions.<br /> <br /> It moves ahead a bit to Eshuvar accompanying River as they\u2019re on a ship, and he\u2019s noted to employ his anima to keep his clothes free from salt spray, while the two talk about seeing their old friends again (with Eshuvar having a particular desire to see Mathar), and a note on how the two have been able to grow closer over the years for living in the same society; Eschuvar being the one that summoned the neomah to create River\u2019s child with her wife, and River having helped Eshuvar out with his wife when he once pushed things too far. It\u2019s a nice insight into part of a Kinship, a few decades down the line and when most of their glory days are behind them. River also reflects on how Eschuvar has changed and not changed in the intervening years, thinking that getting away from the Isle is lifting a weight from him, which builds effectively from what was put up in his own chapter fiction.<br /> <br /> It returns to Kingfisher Swift in the study of her patrician niece, who expresses concerns that their Exalt being away to stop an Anathema from building power and targeting their families might keep her away from helping out if the Dynasty finally goes to war, while Swift (trusting an assessment given by River) assures her that the Houses are unlikely to start making moves before the Solar acts, which her niece agrees with, but expresses a hope that returning to help in an uprising will take priority if it happens before stopping their quarry. I like <i>this</i> one both for continuing a prior narrative, and for one of the Kin being torn between obligations, rather than feeling a relief to go on an adventure.<br /> <br /> Cut to Mathar on the road out of Lookshy, greatly looking forward to seeing his Hearth again, before being accosted by a fellow Lookshyan Exalt, one Vaya, who effectively sabotaged his career in the intelligence service by bringing attention to how he had varied contacts (many in the Realm) that he wouldn\u2019t draw upon. She\u2019s also trying to convince him to not place loyalty to Dynasts over Lookshy. The cross-cultural nature of the Hearth was always interesting, and I like how we get an image of it causing difficulties for at least one member.<br /> <br /> Back to Left Hand Chalima, having fortuitously received the latter informing her of the escaped Solar the day after sending out her own letter inquiring about the Realm\u2019s position on the increase of Anathema activity, and hinting a desire to ride in the Wyld Hunt, with her husband helping her into her armour. They exchange a rather sweet goodbye before she heads off. Don\u2019t really think this has much commentary on an Exalt\u2019s position, it\u2019s just effectively humanising.<br /> <br /> And so the group meets back up in the area where they first came together, now with a town built over it, with River in particular hoping that there\u2019s no trace of the tools that she used to write up the condolence letters that she so hates the memory of. The group catches up, draws up plans, consults a map to determine where the Solar is in a manner reminiscent of their first Hunt. Swift is again the one who suggests that the group should renew their oath, and the group goes so far as to go out and find the actual spots upon which they initially bound themselves, in public view. The fiction ends on a note of River\u2019s contentment at being home.<br /> <br /> As I said at the beginning, the introduction of the signature Hearth was very good for really giving us a group of Exalted with a sense of camaraderie, for basically the first time ever. Seeing how those characters changed in the ensuing time was a good throughline for the chapter fictions, and for them to come together again and go off, dedicated to an important task, gives a nice sense of them transitioning into the uncertainty of the Time of Tumult. It\u2019s actually more than just a good introduction to a group of Exalted, it\u2019s a very effective use of the medium to draw one a bit more into the setting, and help the fictions flow together. It\u2019s a good end to what has been a very well made book; brimming with energy and personality and vivid imagery, that I am sure will prompt many fine games.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T13:03:27-07:00","id":1211948,"title":"","content":"Nagezzar's Manse being called the Throne of Roses actually goes back to his very first appearance in the 1e core, where he inherited it as a byproduct of having poisoned his mother for pitying him unduly for his condition.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,